Subject: [PATCH 0012] PCG: one reduction per DIC-CG iteration with general convergence

Use preconditioned Chronopoulos/Gear CG for every PCG solve selecting DIC,
including ordinary tolerance-controlled solves. DIC's precondition() is a
fixed linear operator for the lifetime of the solve. Keep the original
v2512 PCG loop for every other preconditioner, including GAMG and user-defined
variable/nonlinear operators; no assumption about those operators is needed.
The dispatch is independent of minIter/maxIter and the field name.

Relative to v2512, retain the initial guess, normalisation, initial residual,
outer convergence decision, exact min/max/checkConvergence predicate,
singularity test, residual-field hooks, setFinished and solverPerformance
semantics. Each convergence test still sees sum(abs(r_i))/normFactor for the
residual updated by that iteration, with no lag, squared norm or inferred
residual. The iteration increment and short-circuit evaluation are unchanged:
minIter may force steps past maxIter, and a singularity breaks before updating
psi/r or incrementing nIterations. Floating-point rounding can change the
reported residuals, stopping iteration and breakdown relative to v2512;
this preserves the control semantics, not a universal floating-point match.

Reference: Ghysels & Vanroose, Hiding global synchronization latency in the
preconditioned Conjugate Gradient algorithm, Parallel Computing 40 (2014),
224-238, doi:10.1016/j.parco.2013.06.001, Algorithm 2, lines 4-13 and Eq. (3)
(the preconditioned Chronopoulos/Gear form, not the deeper pipelined form).

Let u=M^-1*r, w=A*u, gamma=r.u, delta=w.u. Reduce (gamma,delta,L1(r))
together with FixedList<solveScalar,3> and sumOp<solveScalar> on the matrix
communicator, as in GAMGSolver::scale. Use p=u+beta*pOld and
s=w+beta*sOld=A*p. First eta=delta, then beta=gamma/gammaOld and
eta=delta-beta*gamma/alphaOld; alpha=gamma/eta. CG conjugacy gives
u.A.pOld=-beta*etaOld, hence p.A.p=delta-beta^2*etaOld
=delta-beta*gamma/alphaOld. With a fixed SPD matrix/preconditioner this is
the same CG iteration in exact arithmetic. The x and r updates remain
x+=alpha*p and r-=alpha*s.

F3 rounding change relative to v2512 (not bit-identical): the direct (A*p).p
reduction is replaced by (A*u).u minus beta*gamma/alphaOld, which can suffer
cancellation; s is formed by a vector recurrence instead of directly
evaluating A*p. The p, x and r recurrences therefore receive different
rounded operands. Packing reductions may also change MPI's summation tree.
The L1 norm is still computed directly from the current residual field.
No reliable equality of stopping counts is claimed for tolerances at rounding
boundaries, or of breakdown decisions near the singularity threshold.

One startup reduction prepares the first step. After each update, prepare
next u,w and pack the next gamma,delta with this step's L1 residual, then
apply the original convergence predicate. An early tolerance exit therefore
leaves one unused DIC application and one unused Amul. Avoid both when the
next iteration is already excluded by the bounds: before incrementing n,
prepare products only if n < maxIter-1 || n < minIter-1. When false, reduce
(0,0,L1(r)) only. This also preserves minIter > maxIter behaviour. An initial
convergence exit with minIter=0 needs neither startup nor iteration work.

An N-step solve without breakdown has N+1 reductions in the new path versus
3N in the old loop, plus unchanged setup reductions. A known final step uses
N preconditioner applications and N iteration matrix multiplies; an early
tolerance exit uses N+1 of each. Two extra local vectors hold u and s. The
reduction is blocking; no nonblocking overlap is claimed.

Relative to the previous fixed-count 0012 (034b3ffd), only dispatch and the
integer scheduling predicate change. For minIter=maxIter=35, the new predicate
(n<34 || n<34) equals the old n<34. Thus startup, each floating-point
expression and operand order, all vector updates and reductions, convergence
calls and singularity tests remain identical, including the norm-only final
reduction. There are still 36 versus 105 loop/startup reductions and exactly
35 preconditioner applications and 35 iteration Amuls, absent breakdown.

With contraction enabled, dot-product sums, s=w+beta*sOld and the unchanged
p/x/r multiply-add/subtract expressions may use FMA on a suitable target.
No flags are changed. Standalone extracted-loop tests with GCC 13.3, -O3,
-march=native and -ffp-contract=off/fast verify bitwise equality to 034b3ffd
at all 35 iterates on four random SPD systems. Convergence-controlled tests
match the original loop's iteration counts and L1 histories within rounding,
including early convergence, maxIter cut-off, minIter overrides, zero
iterations and singularity exits. Production MPI/Cd validation remains an
F3 responsibility; these serial tests are not a cluster result.

